{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And they shall measure to the cities.</b> The Rav Bekhor Shor says that all this the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded to do so that a voice would go out concerning the slain, and as a result witnesses would come and testify that he is dead, and his wife would not remain an agunah. And what our Rabbis said — that they testify only by the face together with the nose, for example if he came and said: I testify about his nose and his face that he is thus and thus, and the witnesses do not recognize him, but from their words those who recognize him would understand that it is this one — but if witnesses come and say: we certainly recognize that this is him, with complete visual certainty as though I certainly saw him alive, his wife may marry on this testimony even after several weeks. And according to this, even from a distant place one may come and examine him. And that case of one who drowned in the sea in the Gemara — ״ואסקוה אגשרא״ etc., and Rava permitted his wife — even though it was after five days, it was ordinary testimony with a resemblance, and he did not say ״I am certain of him.״"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase ואסקוה אגשרא (likely ואסקוה לגשרא, 'they raised him onto the bridge') is rendered as written. The reference is to a passage in the Talmud dealing with identifying a corpse found in water; the tractate is not named in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:01:12.074520316Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}